\v 4 \add Iyov's\add* sons¦232303 \add regularly\add* held feasts in their homes¦232306, and whenever any of them held a¦232305 feast¦232305, he would invite¦232310 all his¦232308 brothers and¦232309 sisters¦232312 to share the meal with¦232316 him.
\v 5 \add Then once a feast was over\add*, Iyov¦232324 would send¦232323 \add for his children\add* and¦232325 consecrate them¦232325—he¦232326 got up¦232328 early¦232326 in¦232327 the morning¦232327 and¦232328 offered¦232328 burnt¦232329 offerings¦232329 for each¦232331 of¦232331 them¦232331, because¦232332 Iyov¦232334 said¦232333, “Perhaps¦232335 my¦232337 \add children\add* have¦232336 sinned¦232336 and¦232338 cursed\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Literally ‘blessed’ but it's possible that the original reading was ‘cursed’ and that scribes changed it to ‘blessed’ in order to avoid the uncomfortable concept of a person cursing God. Traditional manuscripts of the Hebrew Bible don't have a marginal notation about this (as they do in the case of 7:20), but LXX uses ‘ἐνενοήθησαν κακά’ which can be rendered as ‘have thought evil things’ and the Peshitta (Syriac) uses ‘reviled’. Many other translations also use ‘cursed’ since this is the kind of change that scribes are known to have made in similar cases.\f* God¦232339 in¦232340 their¦232340 \add #hearts¦232340\add*.” \add ≈It was Iyov's¦232343 regular habit to\add* \add intercede for his children\add*.
